1
00:00:06,139 --> 00:00:07,207
[keys jangling]

2
00:00:10,744 --> 00:00:13,046
[engine starts]

3
00:00:20,487 --> 00:00:25,091
[Jerry]
This is a 1967 Volvo 1800S.

4
00:00:29,866 --> 00:00:32,500
Volvo's a very straightforward
Swedish company.

5
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:34,900
For example, the official 
Volvo paint-code name 

6
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:37,333
for this color is red.

7
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:43,000
It has a four-cylinder,
115-horsepower engine

8
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:47,100
that goes 109 miles per hour
and will last forever.

9
00:00:47,166 --> 00:00:49,133
A guy who lives
right near where I grew up

10
00:00:49,199 --> 00:00:51,199
on Long Island, named Irv Gordon,

11
00:00:51,251 --> 00:00:53,253
drove one of these three million miles

12
00:00:53,319 --> 00:00:55,488
and he's still not tired of it.

13
00:00:55,555 --> 00:00:58,425
I've been driving it for five minutes,
and I totally get it.

14
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:00,794
I wanna do three million miles.

15
00:01:01,361 --> 00:01:02,429
[phone ringing]

16
00:01:02,495 --> 00:01:03,930
-[Tina] Hello?
-[Jerry] Hello?

17
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:05,598
-[Tina] Hello?
-[Jerry] Tina?

18
00:01:05,665 --> 00:01:07,467
-[Tina] Jerry?
-[Jerry] How are you?

19
00:01:07,534 --> 00:01:09,235
-[Tina] Good, how are you?
-[Jerry] Good.

20
00:01:09,666 --> 00:01:13,100
Seeing as though we are so close together,
geographically...

21
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:15,300
I was wondering if you would
like to meet me

22
00:01:15,341 --> 00:01:16,409
for a cup of coffee.

23
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,733
-[Tina] Yes, please.
-[Jerry] On my way.

24
00:01:24,284 --> 00:01:25,885
Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld,

25
00:01:25,952 --> 00:01:29,322
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

26
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:32,759
Today my guest is Tina Fey,

27
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:36,100
the first female head writer
of Saturday Night Live

28
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:40,566
and is the creator and star of the
million-Emmy-award-winning 30 Rock.

29
00:01:44,704 --> 00:01:47,440
[Tina] Ooh, it's pretty.
Is this a real seat belt?

30
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:49,633
It is kind of, yeah.
You pull it up like that...

31
00:01:49,676 --> 00:01:54,247
I like that this is just like a metal ring
like you would tie a horse to.

32
00:02:00,066 --> 00:02:01,133
[Jerry] Is that a wig?

33
00:02:01,187 --> 00:02:03,890
Or is that the greatest
hair an old woman has ever had?

34
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:06,326
-Oh, that might be a wig.
-I don't mind a wig.

35
00:02:06,392 --> 00:02:07,760
-You don't mind a wig?
-No.

36
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,128
[Jerry] I like healthy hair.

37
00:02:09,195 --> 00:02:11,931
I don't care where it's from
or whose it was originally.

38
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:15,702
[Tina] I've been
on the Upper West Side a long time.

39
00:02:15,768 --> 00:02:19,539
If you walk around the Upper West Side,
you will never ever see

40
00:02:19,606 --> 00:02:21,474
a truly good-looking person.

41
00:02:21,541 --> 00:02:24,110
If you're on 14th Street, you may be,
like, "That girl's a model."

42
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:26,679
-[Jerry] Oh, I hate how true that is.
-Yeah.

43
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:31,400
Mister, what year?

44
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:33,353
-[Tina] '67.
-'67?

45
00:02:33,419 --> 00:02:36,156
Can I be on your show so
I can make some little buck?

46
00:02:36,266 --> 00:02:38,366
I'm tired of driving the cab.

47
00:02:38,424 --> 00:02:41,261
[Jerry] Yeah, but this show
is still just more driving.

48
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:46,633
One of the things I like about you,
besides everything,

49
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:48,966
is, like myself, you enjoy eating

50
00:02:49,033 --> 00:02:51,266
-things you're not supposed to eat.
-Yeah.

51
00:02:51,333 --> 00:02:53,799
-Because, it's like little mini vacations
-Yeah.

52
00:02:53,866 --> 00:02:55,866
-in your stressful day, right?
-Exactly.

53
00:02:55,933 --> 00:02:58,566
Nights where I win an Emmy,
and then be like,

54
00:02:58,611 --> 00:03:02,048
"What's my food treat gonna be?"
Like the Emmy was not a treat.

55
00:03:02,115 --> 00:03:05,451
The only reward for anything is food.

56
00:03:10,857 --> 00:03:13,493
[Jerry] I don't know if you get it
or not, but this car is you.

57
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:16,663
-It's sensible, but fun.
-But it's a little fun.

58
00:03:16,729 --> 00:03:19,432
-[Jerry] So you've never lived in LA?
-[Tina] Nope.

59
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:21,399
Because I don't drive.

60
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:23,803
-You don't drive?
-I don't have a license anymore.

61
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:26,739
-You don't ever drive a car?
-Nope.

62
00:03:26,806 --> 00:03:28,741
How long do you think
you can last like this?

63
00:03:28,808 --> 00:03:31,644
What if you have to get somewhere?
What if you have to go get some food?

64
00:03:31,711 --> 00:03:34,981
-Well, it's New York City.
-Is a car, to you, just like an abacus?

65
00:03:35,048 --> 00:03:38,117
"I know people use them, I don't."
Is that what a car is?

66
00:03:38,184 --> 00:03:39,485
This is like Twitter to me.

67
00:03:40,899 --> 00:03:42,333
[Tina] I had to drive
in one of those commercials,

68
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:44,899
They're like, "Just pull up
and you get out and just run out..."

69
00:03:44,958 --> 00:03:47,794
And I was like, "Yeah, yeah."
Not telling them I couldn't really do it.

70
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:51,497
I pulled up and I forgot
to put the car in park

71
00:03:51,564 --> 00:03:53,266
and then got out and ran in front of it,

72
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:55,868
and the whole crew was like,
"Wait, wait, wait!"

73
00:03:55,935 --> 00:03:57,704
I almost ran myself over.

74
00:03:57,770 --> 00:03:59,906
[both laughing]

75
00:04:03,209 --> 00:04:05,211
-[Jerry] Do you like coffee?
-[Tina] I do.

76
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:07,447
-Are you in the mood for coffee?
-Yes.

77
00:04:13,799 --> 00:04:16,200
[Tina] People keep saying,
"You miss 30 Rock?" I'm like, "No."

78
00:04:16,256 --> 00:04:20,593
I wanna have an office that I go to,
and I make a coffee,

79
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:23,496
-work for 40 minutes...
-So far our lives are exactly the same.

80
00:04:23,563 --> 00:04:25,498
...of thinking about something, and then,

81
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:28,201
"Should we walk out to lunch?"
Then you go out to lunch.

82
00:04:28,266 --> 00:04:31,099
Then you're like, "Oh,
the kids will be home from school soon.

83
00:04:31,137 --> 00:04:32,071
Let's wrap it up."

84
00:04:32,138 --> 00:04:33,239
[both chuckle]

85
00:04:44,183 --> 00:04:46,252
[inaudible]

86
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:01,800
We'll leave you to the menu?

87
00:05:01,966 --> 00:05:03,466
We have one more on this end.

88
00:05:03,936 --> 00:05:06,639
Any juice you'd like,
carrot, beet, apple, cinnamon?

89
00:05:06,706 --> 00:05:10,343
[Jerry] "Guanabana/soursop."
I haven't had that in a while.

90
00:05:10,410 --> 00:05:13,446
"Wheat puffs juice"?
You ever heard of that?

91
00:05:13,513 --> 00:05:16,482
A wheat milkshake? Yeah, we better try it.

92
00:05:16,549 --> 00:05:18,084
-Two? Okay.
-Yeah, two.

93
00:05:18,151 --> 00:05:20,586
-A wheat milkshake?
-Is that what we just got?

94
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:21,688
I think we... [laughs]

95
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:26,266
[Tina] Oh, good, we got two.

96
00:05:26,659 --> 00:05:28,528
[Jerry] Everything's different up here,
Tina.

97
00:05:28,594 --> 00:05:31,631
It tastes like something my eight-year-old
would say, "I invented this."

98
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:35,501
-What is it?
-It's wheat puffs. It's cereal and...

99
00:05:35,568 --> 00:05:37,203
-It says wheat puffs.
-Wheat puffs?

100
00:05:37,270 --> 00:05:39,472
[Tina] I'm sure it's just a box of cereal.

101
00:05:49,182 --> 00:05:51,351
-It's cereal made into a beverage.
-[Jerry laughs]

102
00:05:53,052 --> 00:05:56,522
[Jerry] We know what it is.
The question is, why it is.

103
00:05:56,589 --> 00:05:59,225
-To keep the elderly alive.
-[both laugh]

104
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:05,733
So tell me what you would love
to find yourself doing in a year.

105
00:06:05,798 --> 00:06:09,102
I'd like to write another movie.
'Cause I only wrote one movie.

106
00:06:09,168 --> 00:06:11,371
It's hard to have
that much fire in your belly.

107
00:06:11,466 --> 00:06:14,833
You do something like Seinfeld,
you do it once, and it's thrilling

108
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:17,666
and you're kind of in it together,
but once you've done it

109
00:06:17,710 --> 00:06:20,546
and you know how much work it is,
you can't fake that.

110
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:24,117
I mean, like, I'm satisfied.
I never thought I would do that.

111
00:06:24,183 --> 00:06:27,086
-You know?
-That's how I feel. That's how I feel.

112
00:06:27,153 --> 00:06:29,055
-Yeah.
-I don't mind-- I just looked in the lens.

113
00:06:29,122 --> 00:06:30,590
What, am I on The Office?

114
00:06:33,326 --> 00:06:35,962
God damn that little Ricky Gervais, man.
He's got it figured out.

115
00:06:36,028 --> 00:06:38,297
He never makes more than six of anything

116
00:06:38,366 --> 00:06:40,833
and he sells it to the entire world.

117
00:06:41,166 --> 00:06:42,933
-He writes like six episodes.
-Yeah.

118
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,533
And he's like, "I'm a genius."

119
00:06:44,604 --> 00:06:46,839
Wait, how many episodes of Seinfeld
did you write?

120
00:06:46,906 --> 00:06:48,574
-180
-180, yeah.

121
00:06:48,641 --> 00:06:51,110
But that thing is where season is like 13.

122
00:06:51,177 --> 00:06:54,347
Louis does that. Louis does, like,
13 and then he takes two years off.

123
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:55,633
Oh, yeah. Come on.

124
00:06:55,681 --> 00:06:57,583
'Cause when the... the 22 is...

125
00:06:58,151 --> 00:07:01,354
you start to... Sometimes it's the middle
where you start to go crazy

126
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:03,122
-Yeah.
-Sometimes good things come out.

127
00:07:03,233 --> 00:07:04,833
-Weird episodes.
-Yeah, right.

128
00:07:04,933 --> 00:07:07,099
But, also, sometimes you get pneumonia.

129
00:07:07,193 --> 00:07:10,797
It was the back nine,
was the backbreaker for us right?

130
00:07:10,863 --> 00:07:11,931
Yeah the back nine.

131
00:07:11,998 --> 00:07:14,600
You could kind of figure out
the first ten over the summer.

132
00:07:14,667 --> 00:07:17,703
-You pull three more out of nowhere.
-Right. Yep.

133
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:20,506
-And then the back nine is just...
-I know... [blows raspberry]

134
00:07:20,573 --> 00:07:22,074
You're Sandra Bullock in Gravity.

135
00:07:22,141 --> 00:07:24,510
Yeah. [chuckles]

136
00:07:29,048 --> 00:07:30,983
I always wish I could be a stand-up.

137
00:07:31,050 --> 00:07:34,287
-I tried it, like, three times in Chicago.
-Oh, really?

138
00:07:34,487 --> 00:07:36,389
[Tina]
I remember, compared to improv,

139
00:07:36,455 --> 00:07:39,725
it was so much scarier,
and so much more thrilling,

140
00:07:39,792 --> 00:07:42,528
'cause if you did get a laugh,
it was all yours.

141
00:07:42,595 --> 00:07:44,964
-Right.
-But then when you were flop-sweaty

142
00:07:45,031 --> 00:07:46,365
that was also all you.

143
00:07:46,432 --> 00:07:47,567
The thing about stand-up,

144
00:07:47,733 --> 00:07:51,103
it's a bit of a brutality
that you have to have.

145
00:07:51,170 --> 00:07:53,539
I would venture to say
you're missing that piece.

146
00:07:53,606 --> 00:07:55,341
That reminds me of
when I first moved here.

147
00:07:55,433 --> 00:07:57,133
My mom would say,
"Your cousin has a job

148
00:07:57,176 --> 00:07:59,345
handing out fragrance in Bloomingdale's.

149
00:07:59,412 --> 00:08:02,014
Why don't you just get a job
being one of the girls

150
00:08:02,081 --> 00:08:04,317
who gives out fragrance
in Bloomingdale's?"

151
00:08:04,383 --> 00:08:06,986
Like, "Mom, are you gonna make me explain
I'm not pretty enough

152
00:08:07,053 --> 00:08:10,289
to hand out fragrance cards
in Bloomingdale's?"

153
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:14,727
My cousin was tall and had blond hair,
and she was good at makeup,

154
00:08:14,794 --> 00:08:16,062
and personable.

155
00:08:16,128 --> 00:08:17,396
I'm none of those things.

156
00:08:17,463 --> 00:08:18,397
[both laugh]

157
00:08:18,464 --> 00:08:20,199
That doesn't come in the comedy kit.

158
00:08:20,266 --> 00:08:22,001
"Tall, personable and good at makeup."

159
00:08:22,068 --> 00:08:24,837
-That person is never gonna be funny.
-[laughs]

160
00:08:29,008 --> 00:08:30,810
-Your youngest one is...
-Eight.

161
00:08:30,877 --> 00:08:33,179
Oh, that's how old my oldest is.
So, I have eight and two.

162
00:08:33,246 --> 00:08:34,614
-Two girls?
-Two girls.

163
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:38,166
Sometimes my daughter would
come to set and I'd be shooting a scene

164
00:08:38,217 --> 00:08:40,753
where I'm just pretending to be
married to Steve Carell.

165
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:43,623
They're immediately like,
"What is this, who is this gentleman?"

166
00:08:43,866 --> 00:08:44,733
[Jerry laughs]

167
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,192
I'm like, "This is Mr. Carell."

168
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:50,663
She was always the same way
with Alec, too. She'd be like this.

169
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,300
And he would be like,
"Hi," so warm to her.

170
00:08:55,368 --> 00:08:57,536
And she' be like, "I don't know about you.

171
00:08:57,737 --> 00:08:59,772
My mother seems to spend
a lot of time with you."

172
00:09:02,642 --> 00:09:04,877
Your kids, where are they on Santa Claus?

173
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:07,813
Well, we're Jewish.

174
00:09:09,448 --> 00:09:11,918
I was obsessed with Christmas trees
as a kid.

175
00:09:11,984 --> 00:09:13,586
I would say to my mother,
"Can we get one this year?"

176
00:09:13,666 --> 00:09:16,166
She said, "No. We can have
a Hanukkah bush."

177
00:09:16,233 --> 00:09:18,966
I go, "Oh, fantastic,
when are we gonna get that?"

178
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:20,693
And she says, "Well, it's out front."

179
00:09:20,866 --> 00:09:21,766
Yeah.

180
00:09:21,861 --> 00:09:24,030
I'd go, "Where?"
She opens the door, "That one.

181
00:09:24,096 --> 00:09:25,197
That's it."

182
00:09:25,366 --> 00:09:28,433
"Are we gonna decorate it?"
"No, not this year."

183
00:09:28,501 --> 00:09:29,969
"Not this year."

184
00:09:30,036 --> 00:09:32,371
'Cause I find that certain duties
fall with certain parents.

185
00:09:32,438 --> 00:09:34,440
Like, my husband
is more on the barf patrol.

186
00:09:34,507 --> 00:09:36,709
I can do it. He's better at it.

187
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:40,466
I'm in charge of feces.
All the household feces

188
00:09:40,533 --> 00:09:41,866
are my purview.

189
00:09:41,933 --> 00:09:44,666
I like "feces" and "purview"
in the same sentence.

190
00:09:44,717 --> 00:09:45,785
I like that a lot.

191
00:09:49,989 --> 00:09:54,260
Who do you think, of the two of us,
is more opaque to the public?

192
00:09:55,761 --> 00:09:58,230
-I don't know.
-I think you would win that.

193
00:09:58,297 --> 00:10:00,700
I don't ever look
at stuff online about myself.

194
00:10:00,766 --> 00:10:03,266
'Cause, obviously,
it's, like, such a terrible idea.

195
00:10:03,302 --> 00:10:05,771
But I went online the other day
because there was a picture of Amy.

196
00:10:05,838 --> 00:10:07,073
She was doing some nice thing

197
00:10:07,166 --> 00:10:09,366
where she works with Worldwide Orphans.
I was reading the thing.

198
00:10:09,408 --> 00:10:12,545
And I'm like, "This is so nice."
There's always one person that says,

199
00:10:12,612 --> 00:10:15,848
"I love her. She's the opposite
of Tina Fey, who seems like

200
00:10:15,915 --> 00:10:19,118
she'd never help another person
if her life depended on it."

201
00:10:19,318 --> 00:10:23,723
What? What is it? Is it my face?
Why do you think that?

202
00:10:23,789 --> 00:10:25,925
You should have to have a license
to be on Twitter.

203
00:10:25,992 --> 00:10:27,426
When 30 Rock ended, they were like,
"What do you want?"

204
00:10:27,493 --> 00:10:29,962
"I wanna be in charge
of the Twitter licenses."

205
00:10:30,262 --> 00:10:32,098
So, how would you get the license?

206
00:10:32,164 --> 00:10:34,133
You would give me 10 samples

207
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:35,668
of what you might want to talk about,

208
00:10:35,735 --> 00:10:38,404
and I'd talk to you about whether
that was something we needed to know.

209
00:10:40,172 --> 00:10:42,475
Why is it that everyone
needs to have an audience

210
00:10:42,541 --> 00:10:45,177
for, like, every moment of their life?

211
00:10:45,244 --> 00:10:48,614
The question you're asking is,
"I picked up a rock the other day

212
00:10:48,681 --> 00:10:51,050
and there were all these insane insects

213
00:10:51,117 --> 00:10:53,686
-and I yelled, 'What are you all doing?'"
-[laughs]

214
00:10:53,753 --> 00:10:55,888
-"Stop doing that!"
-"Get organized.

215
00:10:55,955 --> 00:10:58,024
-And behave!"
-"Be birds!"

216
00:10:58,090 --> 00:10:59,959
As if it's an intellectual choice.

217
00:11:01,994 --> 00:11:03,696
[Tina] There was a guy in college
I sort of dated.

218
00:11:03,766 --> 00:11:05,333
I remember going to lunch with him.

219
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,233
He was like, "You, uh,
you really eat a lot."

220
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:10,703
And I just took it as a compliment.

221
00:11:16,275 --> 00:11:18,077
[Jerry]
Have you ever had a Cronut?

222
00:11:18,144 --> 00:11:20,579
Oh, I've heard about these,
I've read about the Cronut.

223
00:11:20,646 --> 00:11:21,981
-Is it good?
-Yeah.

224
00:11:22,048 --> 00:11:25,518
So, I found out,
there's a place in the village,

225
00:11:25,584 --> 00:11:27,253
they have one Cronut left.

226
00:11:27,319 --> 00:11:28,721
-Are you serious?
-I'm serious.

227
00:11:28,788 --> 00:11:32,291
-It may be dented, I don't know.
-I would be interested in any of that.

228
00:11:32,758 --> 00:11:35,561
Great. Let's go downtown.
Where the good-looking people are.

229
00:11:39,331 --> 00:11:40,366
[Jerry] Now, look at this.

230
00:11:40,566 --> 00:11:43,769
[Tina] See, something you will not see
on the Upper West Side.

231
00:11:43,836 --> 00:11:45,638
A model posing in a Porsche.

232
00:11:45,733 --> 00:11:48,199
That's a fake Porsche Spyder.

233
00:11:48,266 --> 00:11:50,400
So, they're doing their show with a car

234
00:11:50,466 --> 00:11:52,533
as we drive by doing our show with a car.

235
00:11:53,212 --> 00:11:54,480
[both chuckle]

236
00:11:57,049 --> 00:12:00,386
[Jerry] What do you buy
that's too expensive for what it is?

237
00:12:00,453 --> 00:12:04,323
-What would be, like, an indulgence?
-An indulgence? Uh...

238
00:12:06,058 --> 00:12:07,560
-I don't know...
-You're squirming.

239
00:12:07,626 --> 00:12:10,162
I'm squirming, I don't have that many!

240
00:12:10,366 --> 00:12:13,500
-How can I answer this and stay opaque?
-Yeah. [chuckles]

241
00:12:20,239 --> 00:12:22,775
There it is. Dominique Ansel,
that's the guy.

242
00:12:22,842 --> 00:12:23,676
[Tina] Your church.

243
00:12:28,633 --> 00:12:31,166
[Jerry] Hello, Dominique.
It's nice to meet you.

244
00:12:31,217 --> 00:12:32,651
-I'm Jerry. This is Tina.
-[Dominique] Hi.

245
00:12:32,852 --> 00:12:34,820
[inaudible]

246
00:12:36,589 --> 00:12:39,492
-Is there something you wanna try here?
-Everything, yeah.

247
00:12:39,558 --> 00:12:42,094
-[Tina] Call an ambulance in 15 minutes.
-[Jerry laughs]

248
00:12:42,161 --> 00:12:43,963
Make sure he's got an insulin pack.

249
00:12:51,504 --> 00:12:52,905
[Jerry] Wow!

250
00:12:52,972 --> 00:12:54,006
Jesus.

251
00:12:58,544 --> 00:13:01,781
All right, here we go.
"She was never seen again."

252
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:10,099
Is there gonna be chains of Cronut stores?
I wonder how he's gonna...

253
00:13:10,156 --> 00:13:11,390
That's interesting,

254
00:13:11,457 --> 00:13:13,159
'cause we know what
any American would do.

255
00:13:13,225 --> 00:13:14,960
-Wreck it. Just blow it out.
-Blow it out.

256
00:13:15,027 --> 00:13:16,629
Pour it every which way but loose.

257
00:13:16,695 --> 00:13:20,065
But he's French, so instead,
he will take a 70-week vacation...

258
00:13:20,132 --> 00:13:21,333
[both chuckle]

259
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:22,935
...and never make it again.

260
00:13:27,439 --> 00:13:29,942
I love that story of the date.
What did the guy say?

261
00:13:30,009 --> 00:13:33,312
He was like, "You really eat a lot."
And I took it as a compliment, like,

262
00:13:33,379 --> 00:13:35,915
"He knows I'm really
kind of real," like...

263
00:13:35,981 --> 00:13:37,783
And did you go out
on a second date with him?

264
00:13:37,866 --> 00:13:41,066
I never went on a real date with him.
It was always just, like,

265
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,389
"You're around. Wanna get a sandwich?"

266
00:13:43,455 --> 00:13:45,558
It was exactly what you hope your daughter

267
00:13:45,624 --> 00:13:47,827
has too much dignity
to ever participate in.

268
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:49,133
I bet you all the women,

269
00:13:49,195 --> 00:13:51,630
and you certainly know
more comedy women than I do.

270
00:13:51,697 --> 00:13:55,668
It's a struggle for them
to balance their feminine projections

271
00:13:55,733 --> 00:13:57,699
with their comedic goals.

272
00:13:57,766 --> 00:14:00,166
Like, if I do a talk show,

273
00:14:00,233 --> 00:14:04,366
I gotta have my stories together,
and I got to try to look okay.

274
00:14:05,077 --> 00:14:05,978
Yeah.

275
00:14:06,645 --> 00:14:09,882
But some people work in coal mines,
so, actually...

276
00:14:09,982 --> 00:14:13,219
[Jerry] I wonder if there are any really
progressive coal mines.

277
00:14:13,552 --> 00:14:14,920
[Tina] We wanna be like Google.

278
00:14:14,987 --> 00:14:16,789
We want people
to have fun on their breaks.

279
00:14:16,866 --> 00:14:19,433
-We're gonna have ping-pong.
-Yoga.

280
00:14:19,500 --> 00:14:20,866
And then a cave-in.

281
00:14:20,926 --> 00:14:22,895
[both laugh]

282
00:14:30,502 --> 00:14:33,072
-Oh, this is the greatest show ever.
-[laughs]

283
00:14:33,138 --> 00:14:35,441
I would commit to 22 episodes
a year of this.

284
00:14:35,507 --> 00:14:36,375
Yeah.

285
00:14:50,966 --> 00:14:53,666
[Tina]
I don't have a key to my apartment.

286
00:14:53,726 --> 00:14:57,162
'Cause we're in that kind of building
where the doorman lets you up.

287
00:14:57,229 --> 00:14:59,865
Tina Fey, no key, no car.

288
00:14:59,932 --> 00:15:01,500
Mental child.

289
00:15:03,435 --> 00:15:06,405
Like this girl. She's not thinking,
"How am I gonna get home?"

290
00:15:06,472 --> 00:15:10,476
"I'll just keep throwing these pillows.
I can make as big a mess as I want."

291
00:15:10,542 --> 00:15:14,346
"I'm adorable. I'm eight foot tall
and adorable."

292
00:15:14,413 --> 00:15:17,449
[Jerry] "I'm gonna spray some cards
and hand them out at Bloomingdale's."

293
00:15:17,516 --> 00:15:20,386
[Tina] She could book that job
at Bloomingdale's like that.

294
00:15:27,126 --> 00:15:29,895
[Tina] One time, I did a two-day
rewrite of a movie.

295
00:15:29,966 --> 00:15:32,633
It was Chris Rock and he wanted
to bring in a few comedy writers.

296
00:15:32,698 --> 00:15:34,700
-Yeah.
-It was me and Louis,

297
00:15:34,767 --> 00:15:36,135
a couple of Chris' friends...

298
00:15:36,201 --> 00:15:38,504
But I just remember
Louis being really funny.

299
00:15:38,666 --> 00:15:41,033
[Jerry]
I'm actually tingling at this moment.

300
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:46,545
Because Louis told me this story,
and you know where he told it to me?

301
00:15:46,699 --> 00:15:50,233
Right there, at Chelsea Pier,
two weeks ago.

302
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:54,000
-This is a good watch for you.
-This is a gift from Chris Rock.

303
00:15:54,066 --> 00:15:56,166
-Oh, really?
-I spent one day

304
00:15:56,221 --> 00:15:59,024
in a hotel suite
with him and some other writers.

305
00:15:59,091 --> 00:16:03,495
He paid us 5,000 bucks each,
fed us really well, and it was just fun.

306
00:16:03,566 --> 00:16:07,266
[Tina] He also gave us each
a beautiful Rolex watch.

307
00:16:07,333 --> 00:16:09,199
[Louise] Inside, it's inscribed,
and it says,

308
00:16:09,268 --> 00:16:10,869
"Thanks for your help, mother [bleeps]."

309
00:16:10,936 --> 00:16:12,404
I don't know if he wrote that
in Tina Fey's.

310
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:13,766
It's engraved, and it says,

311
00:16:13,806 --> 00:16:16,008
-"Thanks for your help, mother [bleeps]."
-Right.

312
00:16:16,166 --> 00:16:19,833
Which, I just like that somebody
had to go to the Rolex store

313
00:16:19,878 --> 00:16:22,114
and ask for five of those
engraved that way.

314
00:16:22,181 --> 00:16:24,149
I almost wore it today, actually.

